WEBVTT

00:00:00.223 --> 00:00:03.052
We will reuse these variables throughout the module.

00:00:03.052 --> 00:00:05.232
Patient ID is an identifier.

00:00:05.232 --> 00:00:07.737
Triage level is an ordered category.

00:00:07.737 --> 00:00:11.512
Temperature is a measurement. Emergency visits is a count.

00:00:11.512 --> 00:00:16.317
Device payload is nested JSON, and a clinical note is free text.

00:00:16.317 --> 00:00:18.587
Notice that the questions are different.

00:00:18.587 --> 00:00:21.382
Some questions are about dataset organization.

00:00:21.382 --> 00:00:23.342
Others are about measurement meaning.

00:00:23.342 --> 00:00:25.402
Others are about numeric behavior.

00:00:25.402 --> 00:00:33.312
Learning to identify which question you are answering is one of the most important skills in this module.

00:00:33.312 --> 00:00:36.787
Connect this idea to a hospital combining patient tables, device JSON, and clinical notes.

00:00:36.787 --> 00:00:42.637
Ask what could go wrong if the concept were misunderstood: could rows be duplicated,

00:00:42.637 --> 00:00:48.332
important records disappear, categories be interpreted incorrectly, or a report become difficult to reproduce?

00:00:48.332 --> 00:00:54.687
A strong analyst can explain both the SQL operation and the assumption behind it.

00:00:54.687 --> 00:00:59.440
After the example, summarize the idea in one plain-language sentence before moving on.
